.bd
DEVICE
.in +5
.br
.bd
ncr
- a SCSI bus on an NCR 5380 host adapter
.sp 2
.in -5
.bd
FUNCTION
.in +5
.br
The
.bd
ncr
device reads and writes disks that speak SCSI, through a host adapter
built on the NCR 5380 bus interface chip.  Its nodes are named for the
bus and not for the card: scsi0 to scsi7 are b 5 0 to b 5 7, and the
target's id is the low three bits of the minor number.  A second
adapter, when there is one, will be a different chip behind the same
bus, and the node names will not have to change with it.

SCSI is a bus and not a drive.  Up to eight devices share it, each with
a number of its own called its id; the card is always the initiator and
a disk is always the target.  The chip knows nothing about disks.  It
holds eight registers the cpu writes and reads, at one port block whose
base is 0x20 - the register number is the low three bits of the
address, so the eight of them answer at 0x20 to 0x27 - and it drives the
wires while the driver tells it when to.  There is no dma controller on
the card, so the cpu moves every byte of a transfer itself, one REQ/ACK
handshake at a time.  A disk has no command register either: a command
is a command descriptor block, the six, ten or twelve bytes that are
the command, handed over a byte at a time in the command phase.

Those eight targets appear again 64 higher, as their whole-disk slices:
5/64 is scsi0c, target 0 with no roll, so that its block 0 is logical
block 0 and reading it reads the disk's label.

Only two commands are built by the driver itself.  One request moves one
block, and the block it sends is a READ(10) or a WRITE(10): ten bytes
naming a logical block address and a count of blocks, with a logical
unit of zero, since a target here has one unit.  Bits 3 and 4 of the
device number name the logical unit for the target's other opens.  There
is no INQUIRY, no READ CAPACITY and no MODE SENSE, so nothing is asked
of a target that it does not have to answer.

The geometry is not part of the name and is not asked for either: it
comes off the disk, from the label
.bd
mkfs
writes into the second half of the boot sector, and the decode is the
same one the other disk drivers here use.  A block number is a file
system block, and it becomes a logical block address through the slice's
offset and length and the volume's roll, so a target keeps whatever
arrangement it was made with.  A target carrying no label cannot be
mapped at all, since the card has no table of disk sizes to fall back
on, and it fails to open with ENXIO - except on its whole-disk slice,
which needs no geometry to find block 0, that being where the label
would be written.

Selecting a target is the driver's work.  The card puts its own id and
the target's on the data bus together - the card is id 7 - asserts SEL
and waits for the target to answer with BSY.  A target that does not
answer is not there, and the request fails.

The transfer itself is a poll.  The driver waits for the chip's REQ bit,
moves the byte, raises ACK, waits for REQ to drop and lowers ACK, and
does that for every byte of the command, the data and the status.  Each
wait is bounded, so a target that stops answering fails the request
rather than holding the machine; the bound is a loop count and not a
calibrated interval, and the wait for a READ's first byte is the one
that costs.  A command that fails is not re-issued: the buffer comes
back with EIO, and reading the sense data a CHECK CONDITION leaves
behind is the caller's business.

The card interrupts when the target lets go of the bus, which is the one
thing it has to say without being asked.  The handler reads the bus and
status register first, since that is the only place the reason survives,
and then reads the reset parity/interrupt register, which is what brings
the card's own line back down.  The driver names the line rather than
numbering it, so which vectored interrupt line the card is jumpered to
is the machine's business and two cards may share one.

The bus is reset when the device is opened, which also serves a machine
that came up with the bus in a strange state.  There is no tick: the
controller answers when it is spoken to.  Requests queue on the card and
not on a drive, since one command is on the bus at a time - a second one
would be talking to whatever target the wires happened to name last.

A command of the caller's own can be issued through
.bd
ioctl (2),
and that is what the six byte commands are for: INQUIRY, REQUEST SENSE,
MODE SENSE and TEST UNIT READY, which a driver with no sense path of its
own has more use for than for its two.  The block is a CDB as it stands
and its length is the caller's, and a command with no data phase costs
no more than the select, the six bytes and the status.

A card that is not in the machine leaves its ports reading 0xff, and a
5380's command register is the cpu's own latch and reads back what was
written to it, which is what tells the two cases apart at boot.  A
machine with no adapter has major 5 answer the way a device that is not
there does, rather than failing every request.
